AWS Cloud Migration Strategies
The 5-Phase Framework
Preparation → Planning → Migrate → Monitor → Optimize
Important distinction on which steps repeat:
Preparation and Planning are one-time activities. Migration, Monitoring, and Optimization repeat for every phase you defined in planning.
Preparation ──► Planning ──► Phase 1: Migrate ──► Monitor ──► Optimize
                                    │
                                    ▼
                             Phase 2: Migrate ──► Monitor ──► Optimize
                                    │
                                    ▼
                             Phase 3: Migrate ──► Monitor ──► Optimize

Phase 1 — Preparation
If you are running a monolithic architecture, you must break it into microservices first before attempting any cloud migration. The reason is simple — containers (which microservices run in) are portable and can be deployed on any orchestrator on any cloud.
If you already have microservices, this step is already done.

Phase 2 — Planning
Planning has two parts.
Part A — Define your phases
You cannot migrate 200 services at once. You divide them into phases based on criticality:
PhaseServicesRiskPhase 1Least criticalLow — use this for proof of conceptPhase 2ModerateMediumPhase 3Most critical (payments, transactions, auth)High — migrate last when you have confidence
You always start with less critical services so mistakes in early phases do not hurt your business.
Part B — Choose migration strategy for each service
This is called the 7 Rs of Migration.
The 7 Rs — Migration Strategies
1. Rehost (Lift and Shift)
Take exactly what you have on-prem and move it to cloud without any changes.
Example — you have 10 services running on a Kubernetes cluster on-prem. You create an EC2-based Kubernetes cluster on AWS and deploy everything exactly as it is.
No code changes
No architecture changes
Fastest migration strategy
Works well when your services have no hard dependency on the on-prem OS or environment
2. Replatform (Lift, Tinker and Shift)
Same as Rehost but you take small advantages of cloud capabilities without changing the core architecture.
Example — you move your Kubernetes cluster to cloud but now configure:
Multiple availability zones for high availability
Auto scaling groups
Control plane spread across multiple regions
No code changes but you are now using cloud features to improve reliability and performance.
3. Refactor / Rearchitecture
The most expensive and time-consuming strategy. You completely redesign the application to be cloud-native.
Example — if you still have a monolith, this is where you break it into microservices, use serverless functions (Lambda), managed databases (RDS, DynamoDB), and redesign everything to be fully compatible with AWS services.
Use this when you need capabilities that your current architecture simply cannot deliver.
4. Relocate
You have an on-prem Kubernetes cluster and instead of rebuilding anything, you lift the entire cluster to AWS using a managed Kubernetes service.
Example — move your on-prem Kubernetes to:
EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service) — AWS native
ROSA (Red Hat OpenShift on AWS) — if you use OpenShift
This is the most expensive strategy because managed Kubernetes services on cloud cost significantly more than self-managed. Decide carefully based on whether the operational savings justify the cost.
5. Retain
Not everything should move to cloud. Some services are too critical, too sensitive, or have regulatory requirements that prevent cloud deployment.
Example — a bank has 200 services. 30 of them handle core customer data and financial transactions. These 30 stay on-prem. The 170 on cloud connect to them securely through a private network connection (AWS Direct Connect or VPN).
You retain on-prem what you cannot or should not move.
6. Retire
Out of your 200 services, some may be outdated, unused, or redundant. There is no point migrating dead weight.
Simply decommission these services before migration. This reduces your migration scope, cost, and complexity.
7. Repurchase
This is where most people get confused because it is fundamentally different from all other strategies.
All other 6 strategies deal with your own application — you are moving, changing, or keeping your own code.
Repurchase means replacing your application entirely with a third-party SaaS product.
Instead of migrating your own software, you just buy a ready-made cloud solution and throw away your old application.
Your old systemRepurchase withSelf-built CRMSalesforceSelf-built HR systemWorkdaySelf-built email serverGmail / Microsoft 365Self-built analytics dashboardTableau / LookerSelf-built helpdeskServiceNow / Zendesk
Why choose Repurchase?
If your custom-built system does something generic that a mature SaaS product already does better — maintaining your own version is a waste of engineering time and money. You pay a subscription and get a battle-tested product with support, updates, and scaling handled for you.
