StrategyYour codeWhat changesRehostStays sameWhere it runsReplatformStays sameHow it runsRefactorGets rewrittenArchitectureRelocateStays sameInfrastructureRetainStays sameNothingRetireDeletedEverything goneRepurchaseDeletedReplaced by someone else's product

Phase 3 — Migrate
Take Phase 1 services (least critical, as defined in planning) and migrate them to cloud using the strategy you assigned to each service. Execute, test, and stabilize before moving to Phase 2.

Phase 4 — Monitor
After migrating each phase, do not immediately move to the next. Monitor it properly:
Give access to internal teams first
Then open to beta customers
Watch for performance issues, errors, latency spikes
Validate that the service behaves identically to on-prem
Only when monitoring shows stability do you proceed to the next phase.

Phase 5 — Optimize
After all phases are complete, you evaluate the full picture:
Was cost reduced? By how much?
Is maintenance overhead lower?
Is performance better?
Are there further improvements possible?
Example — if you achieved 40% cost reduction, you set a new target of 50%. You create Jira tickets for each optimization goal and work toward them systematically.
This is a one-time evaluation after full migration is complete, not an ongoing loop — though you may revisit it periodically as your usage grows.


Can't we just use Monolith architecture:

Yes, absolutely this is a valid case. Converting monolith to microservices is not mandatory for cloud migration.
Why it was mentioned as a first step
That was described in the context of Refactor/Rearchitecture strategy. If you choose that strategy, yes you break the monolith. But refactor is just one of the 7 Rs — you are not forced to use it.
You can migrate a monolith directly to cloud
Several strategies from the 7 Rs work perfectly fine with a monolith as-is.
Rehost — Take your monolith running on an on-prem server and move it to an EC2 instance on AWS. Zero code changes. Zero architecture changes. It runs exactly the same way, just on cloud hardware.
Replatform — Move your monolith to cloud and take small advantages like auto scaling, managed database (RDS instead of self-managed MySQL), or load balancer in front. Still no code changes, still a monolith.
Repurchase — Replace your monolith with a SaaS product entirely. You never had to break it into microservices — you just threw it away.
Retain — Keep your monolith on-prem completely. No migration at all.
Retire — Decommission it. No migration needed.

So when do you actually need to break the monolith?
Only in one scenario — when you choose Refactor/Rearchitecture.
You choose Refactor only when
Your monolith cannot scale to the level you need
Your monolith is too tightly coupled and deployments are painful
You want independent teams owning independent services
You want to use cloud-native features that a monolith architecture cannot support
Your monolith is built on outdated technology that needs a complete rewrite anyway

Real world example
A mid-sized company has a monolith running on their own servers for 10 years. They want to move to AWS to reduce hardware maintenance cost.
They do not need to break it into microservices. They simply:
Spin up an EC2 instance with same OS and specs
Deploy the monolith on it
Point their domain to the new server
Done — that is Rehost
They saved hardware cost, gained cloud reliability, and touched zero lines of code.

The corrected preparation step
The preparation step should not say "you must convert monolith to microservices." It should say:
Understand your current architecture and decide which of the 7 Rs fits each application. If you choose Refactor, then breaking the monolith is part of that strategy. Otherwise it is not required at all.

Database Migration
When migrating to cloud, databases need special attention compared to application services. An application going down for a few minutes is acceptable. A database going down or losing data is never acceptable.
Two things you must handle
1. Choose the right cloud database service
You cannot just pick any database service on AWS. You need to find the right fit for your existing database engine and workload.
AWS offers multiple managed database services under RDS (Relational Database Service):
Your current DBAWS equivalentMySQLRDS for MySQLPostgreSQLRDS for PostgreSQLOracleRDS for OracleSQL ServerRDS for SQL ServerMySQL / PostgreSQL (high performance)Amazon Aurora
Why use RDS instead of installing DB on EC2?
If you install your database manually on an EC2 instance, you are responsible for everything — backups, patching, replication, failover. RDS handles all of that for you automatically. This is the Replatform strategy applied to your database.
However, if your database has very specific OS-level configurations or custom extensions that RDS does not support, then installing on EC2 (Rehost) may be your only option.

2. Backup strategy before migration
This is the most critical part. Before you migrate anything, you need a safety net.
The core principle is simple:
Your production database on-prem keeps running normally. You create a live synchronized copy of it and use that as your backup during migration.

How to implement this
Step 1 — Create a backup database in a separate environment before you begin migration. This can be on cloud or a separate on-prem environment. It must be a live copy, not a static snapshot, because your production data keeps changing during migration.
Step 2 — Keep it continuously synchronized with production using database replication. Every write that happens on production must reflect on the backup in real time or near real time.
Step 3 — Never touch the original production database during migration. You migrate to a new database, not the existing one.
Step 4 — Test the migrated database thoroughly before switching customers to it.
Step 5 — Switch traffic to the new cloud database only when you are fully confident.
Step 6 — Keep the backup running for a defined period even after successful migration. If any issue appears days later, you can roll back instantly.

What happens if migration fails?
Because your backup database was continuously synced with production throughout the entire migration process, it has up-to-date data. You simply redirect your customers to the backup database and they experience zero data loss.
Migration goes wrong

Redirect customers to backup DB
Customers continue normally
Zero data loss
Zero downtime
This is called a rollback plan and for databases it is non-negotiable. Every database migration must have one defined before migration begins.

Key rule
Never start database migration without a live synchronized backup and a tested rollback plan. Applications can be redeployed in minutes. Lost or corrupted data cannot be recovered.

